{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1482591-las-cortes-de-latinoam\u00e9rica","id":1482591,"title":"Las Cortes de Latinoam\u00e9rica","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-06-01T05:26:05-07:00","id":1482591,"title":"Las Cortes de Latinoam\u00e9rica","content":"Has anyone written up Courts specifically for Latin America? In temperate and heavily Hispanicized countries (e.g. Chile) the traditional four-season Court system might make sense (at least in the big cities), but in much of South and Mesoamerica it just feels out of place. Has anyone devoted any thought to how Spanish-speaking New World changelings might organize themselves?<br /> <br /> I seem to remember that another user (can't recall his name) wrote up two courts for Puerto Rico, based on the dry and rainy seasons, but I have no idea how to find out who it was. If you're reading this, would you mind (re)sending me your work?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/856697-motor-city-breakdown-a-playable-changeling-setting-for-detroit-michigan\" target=\"_blank\">Motor City Breakdown</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/19D2lK7otRprpHsJz0aZF0WucpMtoEgGQ2ExZJ-ud0a4/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\">Devil's Night in the D</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1uFgCI8m4gJ5kg1DneG0Cgu18bmUpe4RxDNDfI824G6M/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\">The Magical History Tour</a><br /> Accuracy Consultant on <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/AshesOfTheMotorCity.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Ashes of the Motor City</a><br /> Editor, Compiler, and Senior Contributor to <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1OxlnZCafIZtgPMCfikl0DJvWAwZKJDLk6lxh9hFPB1g/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Tenebrous Seas</a><br /> <b>Current Project(s)</b>: <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/15v9c9IWq_HOjZoYPWqceTxQ0-z1epdN4pt5F6nMUYpE/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\">We Have Fought the Long Defeat</a>, a Late Antiquity/Early Medieval Dark Era for <i>Genius: The Transgression</i>; <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/12IQd13VQ0kHFY19yUBBkqVA-cKT0gZlByOkgYRkb-Ss/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\"><i>Just Before the Dawn</i></a><i>,</i> a 1970s PtH Dark Era","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6327-super_dave","name":"Super_Dave","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6327_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-01T08:13:14-07:00","id":1482606,"title":"","content":"I mean, it sounds hard. It's an area that covers a continent and a half at least basically, with lots of internal political strife and divisions that most northerners don't really understand. On top of the cultures there that take their language and some culture from Portugal or France instead of Spain, there's the influence of pre-Columbian native languages, cultures, and beliefs too, that also vary even within the larger countries, as just like in other places, the people who decided the current political boundaries usually ignored the existing history of the area, splitting up some groups across borders and accidentally forcing long-time enemies into the same polity.<br /> <br /> At the same time, the climate also varies. Sure, some places closer to the equator are two-season. Other places are mountainous, others are jungles, and others have a standard 4 seasons, just in different months, despite sharing the same holiday calendar. On top of that, there are unexpected ethnic enclaves in various places too, both immigrant and multi-generational, ranging from Jews to Slavs to the Chinese.<br /> <br /> Basically, even if they share a language, I think Latin America is too big and complex to cover in even half a dozen court systems. Some places could support full Seasonal Freeholds that just run different periods of the year than usual. Some places might have some rainy/dry model. Some places could even have Directional Courts. I think maybe you need to focus in on somewhere more specific than that whole sphere to find a coherent narrative to latch on to. Maybe just the former extent of the Aztec empire, or the Andes, or just Central America.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>A god is just a monster you kneel to. </b>- ArcaneArts, Quoting &quot;Fall of Gods&quot; <div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/pub/6.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7920-master-aquatosic","name":"Master Aquatosic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7920_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2369"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-01T09:46:58-07:00","id":1482612,"title":"","content":"I am from Brazil and in my specific region we tend to see seasons more as rain and dry.<br /> I ended making 3 courts: Rain, Dry, and Drought.<br /> As droughts are very culturally and historically important in my region.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27153-lunalupa","name":"LunaLupa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27153_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-03-20T17:57:01-07:00","id":1505980,"title":"","content":"Peruvian here, from a young age kids here are taught the 3 regions of Peru &quot;Costa, Sierra y Selva&quot; (Coast, Mountain Range and Jungle). Feels like those could be the courts of Peru, maybe adding a fourth one for &quot;El mar peruano&quot; (Peruvian sea) or just keeping it to those 3","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27830-cabasho","name":"Cabasho","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-14T07:54:14-07:00","id":1554361,"title":"","content":"Retaking this idea.<br /> <br /> <br /> I go with Rainy and Dry Seasons, but mine are just a rework of part Autumn plus Spring verdant effects due to the water (Rainy) and Summer (Dry).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4967,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","user":"lbeaumanior","date":". 10-15-2025, 12:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-14T13:14:22-07:00","id":1554364,"title":"","content":"What if the Latin American courts aren't seasonal but based on the hours of the day? Also the calendar of Saints Days and Liturgical observances might be important with a dozen micro seasons depending on the local festivals, holidays, and Saints Days.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-15T09:24:21-07:00","id":1554377,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1482591-las-cortes-de-latinoam\u00e9rica?p=1554364#post1554364\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What if the Latin American courts aren't seasonal but based on the hours of the day? Also the calendar of Saints Days and Liturgical observances might be important with a dozen micro seasons depending on the local festivals, holidays, and Saints Days.</div> </div> </div> </div>Seems... Inelegant from a design stand point, and impossible from a verisimilitude angle: changelings are rare, but now you have enough of them that you get sufficient members for each of the 24 hour courts to have full time participants? Even worse with the calendar holidays, numbers are simply not adding up.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4967,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","user":"lbeaumanior","date":". 10-15-2025, 03:35 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-15T16:45:07-07:00","id":1554383,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>lbeaumanior</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1482591-las-cortes-de-latinoam\u00e9rica?p=1554377#post1554377\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Seems... Inelegant from a design stand point, and impossible from a verisimilitude angle: changelings are rare, but now you have enough of them that you get sufficient members for each of the 24 hour courts to have full time participants? Even worse with the calendar holidays, numbers are simply not adding up.</div> </div> </div> </div>I was thinking more on the lines of morning, afternoon, sunset to the middle of the night, middle of the night to dawn.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-16T10:18:52-07:00","id":1554389,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1482591-las-cortes-de-latinoam\u00e9rica?p=1554383#post1554383\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I was thinking more on the lines of morning, afternoon, sunset to the middle of the night, middle of the night to dawn.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah I was misunderstanding you. <br /> <br /> I think the Dusk and Dawn courts from first edition have a well thought conceptual framework for something similar. You could take inspiration from those.<br /> <br /> As someone residing on a Latam country I would tell you the real differences here for people are Morning (including really early the-sun-is-not-up), Afternoon, Night; the latter being the longest time.<br /> <br /> So Night the longest, Morning the next one, and Afternoon the shortest. It would have each one with defined thematic roles.<br /> <br /> <br /> Personally, Dawn and Dusk seem better for places where the sunlight and nighttime last the same amount. The manichean duality makes more sense, more even considering that Night is the time for dancing, adult partying, but also crime is more likely.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-16T14:24:32-07:00","id":1554394,"title":"","content":"Courts based on the Liturgical Year would be good too. The Courts would have more changeable populations. Neither the penance of Lent nor the Frenzy of Carnival could be sustained for a lifetime. Although the meditative state of Advent might be.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-16T19:13:33-07:00","id":1554397,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1482591-las-cortes-de-latinoam\u00e9rica?p=1554394#post1554394\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Courts based on the Liturgical Year would be good too. The Courts would have more changeable populations. Neither the penance of Lent nor the Frenzy of Carnival could be sustained for a lifetime. Although the meditative state of Advent might be.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, Chronicles of Darkness by design seems to have gotten away from social constructs tied to specific religions with very limited exceptions, such as the Malleus Malleficarum conspiracy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-17T01:55:35-07:00","id":1554403,"title":"","content":"Try this idea, ditch time based Courts as too European and North American, go with many place/biome based courts. Court of the Forest, Court of the Mountains, Courts of the Towns, Court of the Plains, Court of the Waters (rivers, lakes, the seas, etc.) The odd Courts out would be the Court of the Rains (a Court for witches, Seers, mediums, shamans, etc.) and a Court of Festival, the last being both for celebration and to bring the changelings to common places to talk and communicate in terms of safety and peace.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-17T03:29:55-07:00","id":1554410,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1482591-las-cortes-de-latinoam\u00e9rica?p=1554394#post1554394\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Courts based on the Liturgical Year would be good too. The Courts would have more changeable populations. Neither the penance of Lent nor the Frenzy of Carnival could be sustained for a lifetime. Although the meditative state of Advent might be.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is just the Seasonal Courts renamed.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1482591-las-cortes-de-latinoam\u00e9rica?p=1554403#post1554403\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Try this idea, ditch time based Courts as too European and North American, go with many place/biome based courts.</div> </div> </div> </div>Time isn't an European construct, as aren't seasons.<br /> <br /> Changelings traditionally follow a time-based court system because it makes for a natural change in their power, which is theoretically what confuses the Gentry. You know which court is in power because of the time, and as time passes so does their reign, naturally. And being tied to natural markings is also important, because anything that's arbitrary is also closer to the rules of Arcadia.<br /> <br /> At the end of the day, the reasoning isn't tied to any particular culture. Quite the opposite, saying that sounds weird and condescending, besides just wrong. You can make other Court systems for any other reason, as you can have other kinds of time-based systems better suited to a given area. You can even have Four Season Courts with completely changed themes because in some areas you have the same seasons, but they mean other things.<br /> <br /> But there's also another thing: we're still heavily informed by Europe. LatAm isn't in any way, shape or form less influenced by Europe than the US and Canada, I'd actually say we may be even more. The very name &quot;Latin America&quot; comes from our inheritance of romance language and culture, even if this is frequently forgotten by &quot;westerners&quot; (we're actually more &quot;Western Civilization&quot; than the US).<br /> <br /> While it doesn't means we are not also heavily influenced by natives (when not actually natives, of course), my main point is that the Seasonal Courts actually make a lot of sense as a result of our history. Native Changelings probably had their own Courts before the Iberians came, but what happened to those after the many massacres and heavy acculturation process? What kinds of Courts were formed by the mixed Changelings? Which Courts even native Changelings found to join up at certain points in history? What if in a given area you have &quot;traditional&quot; Courts invented by white people out of what they <u>think</u> the original native courts were, and most native Changelings joining christian Seasonal Courts (because most natives are converts nowadays) and natives that actually follow or try to revive their traditions have an uphill battle to make a place for themselves?<br /> <br /> Seasonal Courts make a lot of sense in most of LatAm precisely because it's linked to the Church, and most of Church practice came from seasonal practices from Europe, including most holidays, and overall it makes sense, for a lot of reasons, for us to just adopt the European way in this regard and tweak it to our customs than to just make something from scratch, and for native influences to be subtle instead of dominant. What's important here isn't so much to have proper LatAm Courts (although sure, you can still do it if you really want a given system), but to think how our history affects the Courts, what changes in them, and what this foreign system entails for everyone.\u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-20T18:30:57-08:00","id":1556869,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1482591-las-cortes-de-latinoam\u00e9rica?p=1554410#post1554410\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Seasonal Courts make a lot of sense in most of LatAm precisely because it's linked to the Church, and most of Church practice came from seasonal practices from Europe, including most holidays, and overall it makes sense, for a lot of reasons, for us to just adopt the European way in this regard and tweak it to our customs than to just make something from scratch, and for native influences to be subtle instead of dominant. What's important here isn't so much to have proper LatAm Courts (although sure, you can still do it if you really want a given system), but to think how our history affects the Courts, what changes in them, and what this foreign system entails for everyone.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you're probably right, it makes more sense to tweak the traditional Seasonal Courts than to invent new ones out of whole cloth. Though TBH I've always thought that switching governments four times a year seems like a really inefficient way of running a community, and that dividing them by territory makes more sense (that's how real-world gangs operate anyway: they rule physical territories, not portions of the year). I think I'd need to do more research into South American indigenous conceptions of the seasons, as well as the liturgical calendar (I'm not Catholic) before I'd feel comfortable even proposing basic themes for the Courts. I have to admit though, I <i>do </i>like <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar857_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"857\" data-vbnamecard=\"857\">Astromancer</a> and<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27830-cabasho\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"27830\" data-vbnamecard=\"27830\">Cabasho</a>'s idea for biome-based Courts, which as I've said above do seem to make better sense to me. Maybe in Latin America the Courts function less like branches of a shared social club and more like werewolf packs, where each one patrols its own territory and takes active steps to keep the others off their turf, unless some overarching threat can unite them against a common enemy?<br /> <br /> Clearly I need to do some deeper research: it would probably be a good idea to learn more about not only the history, but the geography of South America itself. I realize that's kind of a massive question, but... any suggestions where I should start reading or watching?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/856697-motor-city-breakdown-a-playable-changeling-setting-for-detroit-michigan\" target=\"_blank\">Motor City Breakdown</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/19D2lK7otRprpHsJz0aZF0WucpMtoEgGQ2ExZJ-ud0a4/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\">Devil's Night in the D</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1uFgCI8m4gJ5kg1DneG0Cgu18bmUpe4RxDNDfI824G6M/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\">The Magical History Tour</a><br /> Accuracy Consultant on <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/AshesOfTheMotorCity.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Ashes of the Motor City</a><br /> Editor, Compiler, and Senior Contributor to <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1OxlnZCafIZtgPMCfikl0DJvWAwZKJDLk6lxh9hFPB1g/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Tenebrous Seas</a><br /> <b>Current Project(s)</b>: <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/15v9c9IWq_HOjZoYPWqceTxQ0-z1epdN4pt5F6nMUYpE/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\">We Have Fought the Long Defeat</a>, a Late Antiquity/Early Medieval Dark Era for <i>Genius: The Transgression</i>; <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/12IQd13VQ0kHFY19yUBBkqVA-cKT0gZlByOkgYRkb-Ss/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\"><i>Just Before the Dawn</i></a><i>,</i> a 1970s PtH Dark Era","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6327-super_dave","name":"Super_Dave","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6327_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-20T19:06:21-08:00","id":1556871,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Super_Dave</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1482591-las-cortes-de-latinoam\u00e9rica?p=1556869#post1556869\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think you're probably right, it makes more sense to tweak the traditional Seasonal Courts than to invent new ones out of whole cloth. Though TBH I've always thought that switching governments four times a year seems like a really inefficient way of running a community, and that dividing them by territory makes more sense (that's how real-world gangs operate anyway: they rule physical territories, not portions of the year).</div> </div> </div> </div>Remember that Courts and freeholds aren't governments so much as support groups and safety nets, that the nominal purpose of cycling Courts is to prevent whoever's in charge from staying in charge too long (whether to confuse the Fae, prevent the monarchs from becoming like the Fae, or other rationales), and that rulership is less important to a Court than upholding its Bargain.<br /> <br /> A freehold with a consistent structure is beholden to <i>that structure</i> moreso than to the year-to-year decision-making of its individual Courts \u2014 the Seasonal Courts are as much about putting people with the right expertise in power at the right time, and while the particulars of a given Seasonal Bargain may vary from place to place the Courtiers of a season are both suited to the time of year and responsible for propitiating the patron of most immediate relevance during its time. In the absence of massive environmental disruption, poorly-defined handoff markers, disastrous incompetence, or malicious relitigation, everybody is tenured and political games don't need to worry about beating out people from other Courts to seize primacy in the freehold so much as making sure the people in line between you and this time next year don't cause problems you'll need to clean up, which ostensibly allows things to regress to a happy medium.<br /> <br /> It's also worth noting that the function of a freehold is mostly a combination of ensuring its members have the ability to get along in mundane society (securing identification papers or vouching for members, being a sympathetic ear to problems you can't talk about with mortals without risking Clarity, etc), maintaining the Bargains that stymie the Fae from taking them back, and giving its members things to do in a fae context to sate whatever yearning for/residual comfort with the beauties and delights of Arcadia their escape has left them with even if much of it is just made up through the magic of pledgecraft. Being the King of Summer is first and foremost a statement of identity, with what the sovereign in question does to attain and live up to that role being something that can be done well or poorly depending on their ability to meet the demands of their Bargain (and other pledges) and align themself with their patron's demands and strong suits \u2014 efficiency is not the core goal and freeholders are usually not living in or providing board at places that fail the burrito test.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-23T22:28:06-08:00","id":1556947,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong> [URL=&quot;https://forum.theonyxpath.com/&quot;</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Super_Dave[/URL]]I realize that's kind of a massive question, but... any suggestions where I should start reading or watching?</div> </div> </div> </div>Depends on your decision of what to do.<br /> <br /> First and foremost, you have to decide what the courts do on your game. As <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/\">Satchel</a> said they're not Covenants, Tribes or Orders, they have nothing to do with gangs, or even with actual control of something, and most of the time they're not rivals, much less enemies, they're part of the same system. They're survivors' networks for mutual support and protection. Anything more will be heavily dependent on local circumstances, not culture. You may tweak that, but that's the standard assumption.<br /> <br /> In a sense, it's less like having four gangs fighting for turf and more like having an organization with four specialized branches that share direction and cycle the president.<br /> <br /> Also, a lot of it is arbitrary anyway. They're not using Seasons because that's a culture thing, they're using seasons because that's a way to clearly establish the rules. Culture only enters this as an afterthought, it certainly guides a lot of details, but isn't either the point or even the main influence. Direction Courts in the book are suggested for places where directions do have roles in thematic constructs that are widely understood there. It's not about dividing the city in four sections, it's about what orientation (and changes in orientation) mean in those places.<br /> <br /> What that means is that you can go for your Biome-Courts if you want, but they would need a rationale and transition scheme behind. They must <b><i>be symbols</i></b> for other things, <i>preferably</i>, but not necessarily, culturally relevant to the area (as it makes them easier for locals to understand quickly when they arrive from their Durance). Transition rules are the trickier part and, again, they're <i><b>the point </b></i>of the system. You may also have a problem with few places having significant patches of distinct biomes in a city-wide area, biomes can stretch for far larger areas that make the distinction pointless for the Court System. Finally, if you can apply the Biome-Courts at all, you can apply them anywhere, including Europe, just as easily.<br /> <br /> You want Biome-Courts? Seasons? Church Calendar? Where to start:<br /> <br /> <u><b>Biome-Courts</b></u>: they're mostly defined by the convergence of four factors: weather, altitude, water sources and vegetation. And given how those things change from place to place, you won't be able to simply map biomes you know to biomes from other places, you'll have to actually research things. Local sources on Geography or agriculture will be your best friends, non-local sources will miss the connection between biomes and their social meaning in the area (even if this is not the focal point of local sources). As concrete spaces, the main cultural aspects of a biome are related to its impact on safety, sustenance, shelter and settlement, and in myth as the domain of different gods, although myth rarely matters much.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Seasonal Courts</b></u>: the key here is first to understand how seasons vary from place to place. Again, you can't simply map the seasons you know to other places, weather cycles are far more varied than that. Where I live, for example, the main defining factor isn't temperature (which <i>does</i> vary, but not outside human living conditions. You can be naked outside all year around and <i>temperature</i> won't threaten you), but humidity: we go from a rainy season where the sky rarely cleans up, to a dry season where we <i>literally</i> get desert-level humidity and respiratory problems run rampant. Most of the cultural impact will come from how the local seasons impact daily life and activities, but again you can get a lot of mileage from studying the local weather cycle from local sources. Most things will fit by themselves once you get through this.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Liturgical Courts</u></b>: this is less daunting than it seems. First because if you're Christian at all you're more Catholic-adjacent than you realize. Second because this is just European Seasonal Courts under a particular lens. The foundation of it is to do exactly what I told you <b>not</b> to do before: map the Classic European seasons (that aren't all that real, either) to the area without any adjustment at first, not even adjusting for inverted seasons if you're going south of the Equator. If you do just that you already have a working model, just name each season after some holiday instead (anything but Thanksgiving). From this basis, actual symbolism and observance is something that varies a lot from area to area and person to person, so you don't have to worry too much about actual Catholic observances unless you want to, everything you learn about the church or local customs will only add to an already solid foundation.<br /> <br /> That's why I think base it on Catholicism is a good choice in a pinch. It isn't just fodder for social/historical themes, it's easy to do with what the game already gave us.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}